---
import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import PipelineDiagram from '@/components/PipelineDiagram.astro'
import TerminalCast from '@/components/TerminalCast.astro'

/*
 * The session is written from the shapes the specification publishes and from
 * nothing else: the catalogue's own field names, the `aspect_notes` array on an
 * ingest batch, the counts `IngestResult` returns, and the note fields
 * `GET /v1/api/traces/{trace_id}/aspects` hands back. A transcript that invents
 * a field teaches a reader to look for one that is not there, which is the
 * failure mode a documented example has that a code sample does not.
 */
const session = [
  { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/v1/api/aspects?project=support"' },
  { out: '"aspects": [' },
  { out: '  { "name": "goal",      "derived": false, "enabled": true  },' },
  { out: '  { "name": "sentiment", "derived": false, "enabled": false },' },
  { out: '  { "name": "behaviour", "derived": false, "enabled": true  },' },
  { out: '  { "name": "outcome",   "derived": true,  "enabled": true  }],' },
  { out: '"limits": { "min_notes_to_cluster": 50, "max_notes_per_pass": 20000 }' },
  { cmd: '# your worker reads the instruction, calls your model, posts the sentence' },
  { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/v1/ingest" -H "Authorization: Bearer $INGEST_KEY" -d @batch.json' },
  { out: '{ "accepted": 1, "rejected": 0, "duplicate": 0 }', tone: 'ok' },
  { cmd: 'curl -s "$MCPD/v1/api/traces/$TRACE/aspects"' },
  { out: '"notes": [ { "aspect": "goal", "definition_version": 3,' },
  { out: '             "model": "gpt-4o-mini",' },
  { out: '             "text": "The customer wanted a refund on a late order." } ]' },
]
---

<Doc
  title="Aspect notes"
  description="What an aspect note is, who writes one and with which model and credential, the four aspects that ship, and the outcome aspect the daemon derives with no model anywhere."
  lede="An aspect is an axis. An aspect note is one sentence recorded against one finished run along that axis. Your process writes most of them, with your model and your key, the daemon writes the outcome notes, and mcpd stores each one as a signal."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>An axis, and a sentence recorded against it</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>aspect</strong> is an axis a run can be described along: what the
    user wanted, how the interaction ended. An <strong>aspect note</strong> is
    one sentence recorded against one trace along one of those axes. The two are
    different kinds of thing and they are stored apart. An aspect is a
    definition, and a note is a recording made against it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Keeping the two nouns straight is worth the effort, because every query
    parameter in this family names one or the other. An aspect carries a name, a
    display label, one line of description and, where a model writes the
    sentence, the instruction handed to whatever produces it. A note carries the
    sentence, the trace it is about, the version of the definition it was
    written against, and the model that wrote it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A note is not a score. A score is a number on a scale it declares, and the
    <code>label</code> on a score is a categorical result from a fixed set. A
    note is prose, and nothing here ever compares two notes by value. When what
    you want is a number you can chart and alert on, record a score instead: see
    <a href="/docs/evaluation" class="link">Scores and feedback</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Who writes one, and with whose model</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd stores the instruction for an aspect and hands it out on request. It
    does not read it, and it does not act on it. Whatever fetches the
    instruction, calls a model with it and posts the sentence back is yours: your
    endpoint, your model, your credential, your rate limit and your bill. The
    daemon makes no model call of its own along the way.
  </p>
  <p>
    That keeps the rule the rest of mcpd holds to. mcpd is not in your agent's
    request path to a model, and writing a note does not put it there either.
    The call that produces a note happens after the run has finished, against a
    trace that is already stored, from a process you started. Delete that
    process and the runs still record; only the sentences stop.
  </p>
  <p>
    A note arrives as a signal, not through a side door. It goes in the
    <code>aspect_notes</code> array of a batch posted to <code>/v1/ingest</code>
    with a key scoped to ingest, beside the spans, logs, metrics, scores and
    feedback for the same trace, under the same idempotency key. A batch
    replayed after a timeout is a no-op that still returns 200, so a worker that
    cannot tell whether its last post landed can send it again.
  </p>

  <PipelineDiagram class="not-prose my-8" />

  <p>
    A note travels those stages like everything else, and the third one is the
    one that matters for prose. A model writing about a run repeats what it
    read, so an address or a card number can reach a sentence that nothing in
    your code composed. The redactor takes it out on the way in, before the note
    is written down. There is no cleanup pass afterwards, because there is
    nothing left to clean up.
  </p>

  <TerminalCast class="not-prose my-8" title="recording a note" lines={session} />

  <h2>The four aspects that ship</h2>
  <p>
    Four aspects ship with the daemon, compiled into it. There is one list that
    says what exists, and no client holds a second one that could disagree with
    it: <code>GET /v1/api/aspects</code> serves that list with the bounds a
    clustering pass runs under, and every screen reads that route.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Aspect</th><th>The axis</th><th>Who writes the sentence</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>goal</code></td>
        <td>What the user wanted.</td>
        <td>Your process, with your model.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>sentiment</code></td>
        <td>The tone they expressed.</td>
        <td>Your process, with your model.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>behaviour</code></td>
        <td>What the agent did about it.</td>
        <td>Your process, with your model.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>outcome</code></td>
        <td>How the interaction ended.</td>
        <td>The daemon, with no model at all.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Those four make up the catalogue, and it is the same for every project. The
    route also publishes
    <code>max_aspects</code>, the ceiling on how many aspects one project may
    have, and it is fixed at 32.
  </p>

  <h2>The outcome aspect is derived, and needs no model</h2>
  <p>
    <code>outcome</code> ships with <code>derived</code> set. The daemon writes
    its notes itself, from four things it already holds: the run's status, the
    error class of the step that failed, the <code>silent_producer</code> flag on
    the cause report, and the divergence watch. No model is called, no endpoint
    is contacted and no key is needed. It writes them during the analysis pass,
    so only when <code>aspects.enabled</code> is set in the config file.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is there to solve a specific problem with this feature, which is that
    the interesting screen is the last thing you reach. Wiring a model, writing
    an instruction and running a worker long enough to clear the floor is a
    morning's work before you have seen the thing you are building towards. A
    deployment that sets <code>aspects.enabled</code> and configures nothing else
    still fills one aspect from the runs it already has, so the pipeline can be
    watched working end to end before anyone spends a token on it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A derived note carries no <code>model</code>, because nothing wrote it but
    the daemon, and its aspect carries no <code>instruction</code>, because there
    is nothing to instruct. Those two absences are how you tell a derived note
    from one of yours without reading the catalogue.
  </p>

  <h2>Switching an aspect off</h2>
  <p>
    Whether an aspect is on is deployment configuration, not a setting per
    project. The <code>aspects</code> block of the config file holds a
    <code>disabled</code> list of aspect names. An aspect named there is off on
    every project, and every other aspect is on. Every catalogue row carries the
    resulting <code>enabled</code> flag, and no endpoint changes it: the read
    API reports the state and does not set it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Switching an aspect off changes what the pass does afterwards and never
    rewrites what is stored. No pass clusters it, its progress no longer counts
    towards readiness, and for <code>outcome</code> the daemon stops writing
    notes. Notes already stored stay readable, and the aspect stays in the
    catalogue. That is why the catalogue returns disabled rows as well as
    enabled ones. A name that vanished from the list would make every note filed
    under it look like a mistake.
  </p>
  <p>
    An aspect can be switched off. It cannot be deleted, for the same reason:
    notes already name it, and a note whose aspect no longer exists is a sentence
    filed under nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Every note names a definition version</h2>
  <p>
    Every note records the <code>definition_version</code> it was written
    against, and the catalogue reports each aspect's <code>version</code>.
    Every built-in aspect is at version 1, so the outcome notes the daemon writes all carry 1. A note your own process posts
    carries the version it sends.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the version when a set of notes surprises you. Two notes along one
    aspect that answer visibly different questions can come from two writers
    that sent different versions, and that is the first thing to rule out.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading what was written about one run</h2>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/traces/{'{trace_id}'}/aspects</code> returns every note
    recorded about one trace, in catalogue order, at most one per aspect. It is
    not entity scoped, because a trace id is unique across the deployment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read it beside the trace itself. This is the shortest route from an
    aggregate back to a run: a note is the sentence that put this trace in each
    of its themes, so it is also the place to check a grouping you do not
    believe. An empty list is the ordinary answer for a recent trace. Writing a
    note lags ingestion, sometimes by a long way.
  </p>

  <h2>What a note proves</h2>
  <p>
    A note proves one thing: that this sentence was recorded against that trace,
    by the definition named in <code>aspect</code>, at the version in
    <code>definition_version</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A note is evidence of what a definition said about a run, and the run is
    what settles whether the definition was right. When the two disagree, the
    trace wins. Read a note as a pointer into the recording, never as a finding
    about it.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is also why the aggregate on the next page groups notes by
    their wording and not by their meaning: mcpd never reads a sentence, at any
    point, in either direction.
  </p>

  <h2>The text is payload, and is treated as payload</h2>
  <p>
    A note is prose about a real conversation, and one a model wrote can repeat
    anything the model read. That makes notes the same kind of material as a
    prompt or a completion, and mcpd handles every note the same way. It passes through the redactor on the way in. Retention sweeps it
    on the same schedule as the trace it belongs to. Erasing a subject erases it,
    because a note inherits its trace's correlation and is scoped, filtered,
    retained and erased exactly as that trace is.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reading notes back needs a read key, and every route that hands back a
    sentence is recorded in the audit ledger like every other endpoint that
    returns what a user wrote. See
    <a href="/docs/privacy" class="link">Redaction and retention</a> for what
    never reaches storage and what stops being stored, and
    <a href="/docs/security" class="link">Security</a> for the scopes and the
    ledger.
  </p>

